## Summary

The layout effect (line 96) is declared before the worker-attach effect (line 151), and React runs mount effects in declaration order. layoutWorkerRef is a module-level ref initialized to null, so on the first-ever mount of <Graph> in a session the effect at line 109 calls layoutWorkerRef.current?.postMessage(...) while the ref is still null. Optional chaining silently swallows the request; the worker effect then creates the worker but nothing re-posts the layout. Result: the first visit to the Graph tab per app launch shows only the jittered seed-circle layout and never refines via Barnes-Hut, until the user toggles a kind filter or the graph refreshes.

## Evidence

- **File**: `src/views/Graph.tsx` (lines 96-124,151-153,685-687)

```
const reqId = ++layoutReqSeq.current;
    layoutWorkerRef.current?.postMessage({ type: "layout", requestId: reqId, ... });
// later, effect declared at line 151:
    const w = ensureLayoutWorker();
    layoutWorkerRef.current = w;
```

## Suggested fix

Create the worker before posting: replace layoutWorkerRef.current?.postMessage with const w = ensureLayoutWorker(); layoutWorkerRef.current = w; w.postMessage(...), or declare the worker-attach effect above the layout effect, or initialize the worker lazily at module scope.

