## Summary

loadMoreMemories reads get().memoryOffset and get().currentProjectId before awaiting, then appends unconditionally after the await. Two rapid scroll triggers both read the same offset and append the same batch twice (duplicate uids). Worse, if the user switches projects while a page is in flight, the old project's batch is appended to the new project's freshly refreshed memories array — mixed-project list content with no visual indication.

## Evidence

- **File**: `src/lib/store.ts` (lines 159-176)

```
loadMoreMemories: async () => {
  const offset = get().memoryOffset;
  const batch = await api.listMemories({ project_id: get().currentProjectId, limit: 50, offset });
  set((s) => { const combined = [...s.memories, ...batch]; ... });
}
```

## Suggested fix

Capture projectId at call time and compare against get().currentProjectId before set(); add an isLoadingMore flag that makes concurrent calls no-op.

